## 10. PERFORMANCE, IMAGE QUALITY, AND CODE SAFETY

- Preload the base hero image and fetch the reveal image promptly.
- Use optimized provided WebP images; PNG originals are optional archival sources, not necessary for page rendering.
- Render the two hero layers within one shared image stage.
- No per-frame React rerenders for cursor tracking; use RAF and direct CSS var updates.
- Never call `canvas.toDataURL()` or `getImageData()` every pointer frame.
- Avoid global `pointermove` listeners unless absolutely needed; use hero-local listeners, properly cleaned up.
- Use GSAP contexts and `revert()` on unmount; register ScrollTrigger only as needed.
- Call ScrollTrigger refresh after layout/image readiness, but avoid loops.
- Handle navigation header offsets for anchor scrolling.
- Do not update layout-affecting properties every frame; prefer transforms, opacity, masks.
- Avoid excessive shadows, blur filters, full-page scroll-driven noise, and heavy JS animation on low-power devices.
- Pause RAF when hero isn't visible.
- Avoid hydration/client runtime issues. Vite use is client-side; the required `'use client';` directive remains in place as specified.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`, `prefers-reduced-data` where useful, and touch targets.
- Test actual asset paths—do not silently substitute base64 placeholders or remote URLs.

If no image-generation capability exists for additional section visuals, make the page polished with real CSS/SVG compositions and the existing pair rather than fabricating stock URLs or showing broken images.

